# timecrate.include — what goes in the recovery kit. Installed to /etc/timecrate/include.
#
# One path per line, relative to / (no leading slash). `~/` expands to the configured user's
# home, so this file stays correct if the username changes. Directories recurse fully;
# timecrate.exclude prunes junk inside them. Blank lines and # comments are ignored.
# A line containing * ? or [ is a shell glob, expanded at every backup (~/*.sh, ~/.app-*.env),
# so files that appear later are picked up; one that matches nothing adds nothing.
#
# The point of this list is the IRREPLACEABLE slice of the box: things you cannot reinstall,
# re-download or regenerate. Packages, caches and media do NOT belong here — a manifest of
# installed packages is captured automatically and replayed at restore.
#
# Edit this file. The defaults below are a starting point, not a survey of your machine.
# Preview what they actually select:   sudo timecrate backup --dry-run

# --- System: small, precious, and outside any home directory ---
etc
root
usr/share/keyrings

# --- Secrets and identity — this is WHY the kit is encrypted ---
~/.ssh
~/.gnupg
~/.gitconfig
~/.config/rclone

# --- Shell and local automation (usually in no git repo) ---
~/.bashrc
~/.profile
~/.config/systemd/user
~/.config/environment.d
~/.local/bin

# --- Desktop and application settings you hand-tuned ---
~/.config/dconf/user

# --- Your documents ---
~/Documents

# --- Uncomment if you use these ---
#var/lib/tailscale
#~/.docker/config.json
#~/.config/Code/User
#~/Pictures
#~/work

# --- OS-restore completeness -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Custom binaries and libraries. By definition these come from no package, so the dpkg selections
# replay cannot recreate them — on the first box audited this was the single largest hole.
usr/local
# Per-user display-manager settings (avatar, autologin, session choice).
var/lib/AccountsService
# The whole config tree, not a cherry-picked list. A hand-picked list means every NEW application's
# settings are silently missed until someone happens to look; the audit that prompted this found
# thirty-plus uncovered directories including autostart, goa-1.0 (online accounts), gtk-3.0/4.0 and
# the desktop tooling. Browser profiles are excluded instead — see the exclude list.
~/.config
# Desktop integration and credential-adjacent state.
~/.local/share/applications
~/.local/share/gnome-shell
~/.local/share/keyrings
~/.local/share/icc
~/.secrets
~/.pki
~/.face
~/.bash_history

# --- the tool itself ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A kit must contain the thing that opens it. Nothing else guarantees it: once the tool is
# installed from a package it lives in /usr, which no other line here names, and a restored
# machine would come up without it -- or with whatever stale copy happened to be lying elsewhere.
#
# ~200 KB. It also removes a circular dependency from disaster recovery: without this, opening a kit
# needs the package archive and a working network, which are part of what the kit exists to
# survive the loss of.
usr/bin/timecrate
usr/bin/timecrate-gui
usr/libexec/timecrate
usr/share/timecrate
